WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.340 --> 00:00:04.880
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.880 --> 00:00:10.430
This glorious paper from about 7 years ago
was about teaching digital creatures to walk,

00:00:10.430 --> 00:00:15.540
the numbers here showcase the process of learning
over time, and it is clear that the later

00:00:15.540 --> 00:00:19.240
generations did much better than the earlier
ones.

00:00:19.240 --> 00:00:23.721
These control algorithms are not only able
to teach these creatures to walk, but they

00:00:23.721 --> 00:00:29.119
are quite robust against perturbations as
well, or, more simply put, we can engage in

00:00:29.119 --> 00:00:34.140
one of the favorite pastimes of a computer
graphics researcher, which is, of course,

00:00:34.140 --> 00:00:39.000
throwing boxes at the character and seeing
how well it can take it.

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:41.600
This one has done really well!

00:00:41.600 --> 00:00:43.910
Well, kind of.

00:00:43.910 --> 00:00:49.320
Now we just noted that this is a computer
graphics paper, and an amazing one at that,

00:00:49.320 --> 00:00:54.070
but it does not yet use these incredible new
machine learning techniques that just keep

00:00:54.070 --> 00:00:56.489
getting better year by year.

00:00:56.489 --> 00:01:01.780
You see, these agents could learn to inhabit
a given body, but I am wondering what would

00:01:01.780 --> 00:01:06.090
happen if we would suddenly change their bodies
on the fly?

00:01:06.090 --> 00:01:08.490
Could previous methods handle it?

00:01:08.490 --> 00:01:10.369
Unfortunately, not really.

00:01:10.369 --> 00:01:15.340
And I think it is fair to say that an intelligent
agent should have the ability to adapt when

00:01:15.340 --> 00:01:16.630
something changes.

00:01:16.630 --> 00:01:21.899
Therefore, our next question is, how far have
we come in these 7 years?

00:01:21.899 --> 00:01:25.959
Can these new machine learning methods help
us create a more general agent that could

00:01:25.959 --> 00:01:31.009
control not just one body, but a variety of
different bodies?

00:01:31.009 --> 00:01:35.639
Let’s have a look at today’s paper, and
with that, let the fun begin.

00:01:35.639 --> 00:01:40.619
This initial agent was blessed with reasonable
body proportions, but of course, we can’t

00:01:40.619 --> 00:01:42.439
just leave it like that.

00:01:42.439 --> 00:01:44.639
Yes, much better.

00:01:44.639 --> 00:01:51.249
And, look, all of these combinations can still
walk properly, and all of them use the same

00:01:51.249 --> 00:01:53.469
one reinforcement learning algorithm.

00:01:53.469 --> 00:01:59.729
But do not think for a second that this is
where the fun ends, no, no, no.

00:01:59.729 --> 00:02:05.059
Now, hold on to your papers, and now let’s
engage in these horrific asymmetric changes.

00:02:05.059 --> 00:02:11.930
There is no way that the same algorithm could
be given this body and still be able to walk.

00:02:11.930 --> 00:02:13.319
Goodness!

00:02:13.319 --> 00:02:14.319
Look at that!

00:02:14.319 --> 00:02:17.100
It is indeed still able to walk.

00:02:17.100 --> 00:02:22.340
If you have been holding on to your papers,
good now squeeze that paper, because after

00:02:22.340 --> 00:02:26.390
adjusting the height and thickness of this
character, it could still not only walk, but

00:02:26.390 --> 00:02:27.540
even dance.

00:02:27.540 --> 00:02:29.750
But it goes further.

00:02:29.750 --> 00:02:33.969
Do you remember the crazy asymmetric experiment
for the legs?

00:02:33.969 --> 00:02:38.489
Let’s do something like that with thickness.

00:02:38.489 --> 00:02:44.269
And as a result, they can still not only walk,
but even perform gymnastic moves.

00:02:44.269 --> 00:02:45.420
Wo-hoo!

00:02:45.420 --> 00:02:50.129
Now it’s great that one algorithm can adapt
to all of these body shapes, but it would

00:02:50.129 --> 00:02:53.940
be reasonable to ask, how much do we have
to wait for it to adapt?

00:02:53.940 --> 00:02:56.629
Have a look here.

00:02:56.629 --> 00:02:59.010
Are you seeing what I am seeing?

00:02:59.010 --> 00:03:04.450
We can make changes to the body on the fly,
and the AI adapts to it immediately.

00:03:04.450 --> 00:03:07.770
No re-training, or parameter tuning is required.

00:03:07.770 --> 00:03:11.860
And that is the point where I fell off the
chair when I read this paper.

00:03:11.860 --> 00:03:13.850
What a time to be alive!

00:03:13.850 --> 00:03:17.700
And now, Scholars, bring in the boxes.

00:03:17.700 --> 00:03:19.440
Ha-haa!

00:03:19.440 --> 00:03:24.390
It can also inhabit dogs and fish, and we
can also have some fun with them as we grab

00:03:24.390 --> 00:03:27.810
a controller and control them in real time.

00:03:27.810 --> 00:03:33.110
The technique is also very efficient as it
requires very little memory and computation,

00:03:33.110 --> 00:03:38.689
not to mention that we only have to train
one controller for many body types instead

00:03:38.689 --> 00:03:42.150
of always retraining after each change to
the body.

00:03:42.150 --> 00:03:47.340
However, of course, this algorithm isn’t
perfect, one of its key limitations is that

00:03:47.340 --> 00:03:52.379
it will not do well if the body shapes we
are producing strays too far away from the

00:03:52.379 --> 00:03:54.550
ones contained in the training set.

00:03:54.550 --> 00:03:58.310
But let’s leave some space for the next
followup paper too.

00:03:58.310 --> 00:04:01.970
And just one more thing that didn’t quite
fit into this story.

00:04:01.970 --> 00:04:07.030
Every now and then I get these heartwarming
messages from you Fellow Scholars noting that

00:04:07.030 --> 00:04:11.560
you’ve been watching the series for a while
and decided to turn your lives around, and

00:04:11.560 --> 00:04:14.359
go back to study more and improve.

00:04:14.359 --> 00:04:15.690
Good work Mr. Munad!

00:04:15.690 --> 00:04:20.560
That is absolutely amazing and reading these
messages are a true delight to me.

00:04:20.560 --> 00:05:16.759
Please keep them coming.

00:05:16.759 --> 00:05:20.300
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

